==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|doubles up|doubling up|doubled up}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[double]] the quantity, amount or duration of something
#: ''I'm going to '''double up''' my enlistment.''
# {{lb|en|poker|by extension|intransitive}} To double one's amount of chips by winning an [[all-in]] pot.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[bend]], bend over; to [[fold]]; to [[stoop]].
#* '''1886''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, {{w|Strange Case Of Dr Jekyll And Mr Hyde}}:
#*: "It is well, then that we should be frank," said the other. "We both think more than we have said; let us make a clean breast. This masked figure that you saw, did you recognise it?" / "Well, sir, it went so quick, and the creature was so '''doubled up''', that I could hardly swear to that," was the answer. "But if you mean, was it Mr. Hyde?--why, yes, I think it was!"
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have a secondary use.
#: ''This unfolding sofa '''doubles up''' as a bed.''
# {{lb|en|baseball|transitive}} To get the second out in a [[double play]], typically referring to getting an out by beating a runner back to a base (often by throwing) after a fly ball has been caught
#: ''Jones snared the [[liner]] and then stepped on the bag to '''double up''' the runner.''
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To employ double the usual resources for a particular purpose.
#* {{quote-text|year=2012|author=Ben Smith|title=Leeds United 2-1 Everton|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19632366|passage=Tactically smart, Leeds' work-rate was also admirable, their players often '''doubling up''' on Everton's main threats like Marouane Fellaini, while Victor Anichibe found he had unwelcome, unstinting company throughout.}}

